WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.272 --> 00:00:15.036
My name is Fillippo Bogliano, and I live in northern Italy.

2
00:00:15.837 --> 00:00:20.078
My home is on the southern slopes of the Alps in a city called Turin, which is also

3
00:00:20.158 --> 00:00:20.879
on the Po River.

4
00:00:22.239 --> 00:00:27.701
Many people travel here to trade with the local merchants for all kinds of products; some buy red

5
00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:30.262
wine while others come for our nutty chocolate.

6
00:00:31.503 --> 00:00:33.804
But Turin is best known for its cheese!

7
00:00:34.484 --> 00:00:35.204
How do I know this?

8
00:00:35.865 --> 00:00:38.986
Because I am a casaro, an expert cheese maker.

9
00:00:40.887 --> 00:00:46.330
My family first came to this area from France in 1745, when I was just a year old.

10
00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:52.953
Then my father herded cattle in the mountains and sold fresh milk, but this was hard work and

11
00:00:52.993 --> 00:00:54.133
did not pay very well.

12
00:00:55.154 --> 00:00:59.636
My father soon discovered that the casaros who bought his milk made a very nice living.

13
00:01:00.424 --> 00:01:04.607
So he decided that when I was old enough, I would learn the cheese-making trade.

14
00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:09.329
When I reached the age of 11, my father sent me to live with one of the most

15
00:01:09.389 --> 00:01:11.090
respected casaros in Turin.

16
00:01:11.791 --> 00:01:16.513
This man was glad to be my mentor since my father had taught me so much about milk

17
00:01:16.593 --> 00:01:22.937
already. I was an apprentice for five years, and I learned everything there was to know about making

18
00:01:22.997 --> 00:01:29.573
cheese. I quickly realized that no two batches of cheese are alike, just as no pail of fresh

19
00:01:29.673 --> 00:01:31.474
milk is exactly the same as another.

20
00:01:32.234 --> 00:01:37.917
I also learned that even a small change in the cheese-making process can result in a completely different

21
00:01:37.957 --> 00:01:38.578
kind of cheese.

22
00:01:39.598 --> 00:01:44.561
Now I am 22, and I have my own business, which keeps my whole family busy.

23
00:01:45.322 --> 00:01:50.785
My mother and father sell our cheese in the marketplace; my two brothers tend the cows, and my

24
00:01:50.845 --> 00:01:52.766
wife, Magda, manages the household.

25
00:01:53.486 --> 00:01:56.688
We all work together because making cheese is difficult work.

26
00:01:57.584 --> 00:02:03.669
Several kinds of cheese are made in this region of Italy, but Gorgonzola is my favorite, because this

27
00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:07.953
white, creamy cheese with blue veins has a sharp, tangy flavor.

28
00:02:08.714 --> 00:02:12.177
It is the most famous cheese in Turin and my specialty.

29
00:02:13.318 --> 00:02:19.783
Legend has it that Gorgonzola was discovered by accident by an apprentice casaro who was very much in

30
00:02:19.883 --> 00:02:24.968
love. He was late for a meeting with his girlfriend, but he had not finished his work.

31
00:02:25.708 --> 00:02:30.531
So he dumped his unfinished cheese into a pail of fresh milk that a herdsman had brought in.

32
00:02:31.412 --> 00:02:38.517
Later the mixture hardened, became moldy, and turned greenish blue, but the casaro didn't want to throw it

33
00:02:38.577 --> 00:02:42.960
away. So he sold it anyway, and his customers loved it!

34
00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.421
Gorgonzola cheese was born.

35
00:02:46.562 --> 00:02:50.565
To be honest, though, making this wonderful cheese takes talent and patience.

36
00:02:51.806 --> 00:02:57.225
Every evening, after our herd comes down from the alpine meadows, my brothers milk the cows and bring

37
00:02:57.265 --> 00:02:58.525
the milk into my workshop.

38
00:02:59.845 --> 00:03:04.247
I carefully pour the milk into shallow pails and allow it to set overnight.

39
00:03:04.947 --> 00:03:08.288
The next day I add a special ingredient called rennet.

40
00:03:09.228 --> 00:03:14.069
Rennet is an acidic enzyme, a natural chemical produced in a calf's stomach.

41
00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:21.211
It turns the milk into a curd—a thickened milk paste—and separates the curd from the whey, a thin

42
00:03:21.271 --> 00:03:22.232
and yellowish liquid.

43
00:03:22.952 --> 00:03:25.714
In fact, this action is called "turning" the milk.

44
00:03:26.494 --> 00:03:30.458
On the morning of the second day, I make another curd from the morning milking.

45
00:03:31.378 --> 00:03:36.202
Then, in the afternoon, I combine the curd from the first day with the curd from the second

46
00:03:36.242 --> 00:03:41.486
day. I put the fresh curd on the bottom and sides of the pail and the older curd

47
00:03:41.526 --> 00:03:42.167
in the center.

48
00:03:43.048 --> 00:03:48.832
Next I gently knead the curd with my hands, smoothing it out, and remove it from the pail.

49
00:03:49.793 --> 00:03:53.216
Then I shape the cheese into a circular shape, called a round.

50
00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:59.304
Many types of cheese are made in this way, and cheese can be made in rounds of any

51
00:03:59.384 --> 00:04:03.287
size—some are small and some are quite large.

52
00:04:04.028 --> 00:04:09.832
I make about three rounds a day, and flip them over frequently to drain the remaining whey out

53
00:04:09.852 --> 00:04:10.413
of the curd.

54
00:04:12.815 --> 00:04:18.059
Most days there is no time to go back to the house for breakfast or lunch, so Magda

55
00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:19.520
brings pasta to my workshop.

56
00:04:20.148 --> 00:04:24.470
She doesn't like to come here very much because she thinks that the cheese is too stinky.

57
00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:28.373
She says that Gorgonzola smells like dirty socks!

58
00:04:29.173 --> 00:04:31.714
She is right; it does smell pretty bad.

59
00:04:32.155 --> 00:04:35.176
But I am so used to it now that it doesn't bother me.

60
00:04:36.037 --> 00:04:38.118
I just think about how good it will taste.

61
00:04:40.819 --> 00:04:45.602
The final step is to add sea salt to the rounds of curd over a period of five

62
00:04:45.662 --> 00:04:47.043
days to firm them up.

63
00:04:47.743 --> 00:04:51.881
Then the rounds are ready to be ripened, and my brothers and I take all the rounds to

64
00:04:51.921 --> 00:04:52.821
a nearby cave.

65
00:04:53.882 --> 00:04:59.204
We stack them on tall wooden racks like books on a library shelf, and as they go through

66
00:04:59.244 --> 00:05:03.726
the aging process, I return to the cave often to flip the rounds over.

67
00:05:04.466 --> 00:05:07.287
This keeps the rounds firm and hard as they age.

68
00:05:08.268 --> 00:05:13.250
Our caves in northern Italy have the perfect temperature and humidity for ripening the cheese.

69
00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:17.852
These special caves also have a mold that grows in them.

70
00:05:18.696 --> 00:05:25.100
It attaches itself to the ripening cheese and, like magic, causes the blue and green veins to appear

71
00:05:25.141 --> 00:05:27.302
in the cheese in about three weeks!

72
00:05:28.483 --> 00:05:33.206
We have to let the Gorgonzola ripen for at least three months before it is ready to eat.

73
00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.468
Younger cheese tastes sweeter and is softer.

74
00:05:37.289 --> 00:05:41.512
Rounds that are aged more than six months are harder and have a spicy flavor.

75
00:05:42.372 --> 00:05:47.736
Older Gorgonzola that is aged for a year is more valuable, so we mostly make this kind.

76
00:05:48.748 --> 00:05:53.549
When the cheese rounds are ready, my brothers carry them to the wagon so that my mother and

77
00:05:53.629 --> 00:05:55.950
father can take them to the market the next day.

78
00:05:56.730 --> 00:06:03.712
At our stall, Bogliano's Gorgonzola, everyone can stop and taste our cheese, drink a little wine, and talk

79
00:06:03.772 --> 00:06:04.752
about the lovely weather.

80
00:06:06.313 --> 00:06:10.734
Oh, I wish I could eat and talk with our customers, but I must stay behind to clean

81
00:06:10.774 --> 00:06:13.915
up the caves and watch over the next batch of cheese as it ages.

82
00:06:14.775 --> 00:06:18.056
My cheese-making work never ends, but I love it!
